# mgbomb.msg - the atomic bomb (mgbomb.ssl).
# 100 to 199 looks, 200 and up the menu and its outcomes (shown as conversation text
# when the bomb is used, in the message window when a skill or an item is used on it).

{100}{}{You see an atomic bomb. It is sitting in a puddle.}
{101}{}{A fat steel casing, taller than you are, nose-down in glowing water. MEGATON runs along its side in stenciled letters a hand high. From behind a rusted access plate comes a slow, patient tick.}
{102}{}{A very large, very dead atomic bomb. The ticking has stopped. The puddle glows on regardless.}
{103}{}{The bomb ticks as it always has. A red lamp winks at the access plate now, and behind it something that was not there before hums along.}

# The outside: dormant, disarmed, rigged.
{200}{}{The bomb looms over you, warm as a sleeping animal. A slow tick comes from behind a rusted access plate held on by four bolts, two of them long gone.}
{201}{}{The bomb is silent. Its access plate hangs open on the last bolt. Inside, the cut wires have been folded back as neatly as a made bed.}
{202}{}{The tick is still there. Under it, behind the plate, something hums: the sound of a thing that has been given a purpose.}
{210}{}{Open the access plate.}
{211}{}{Trace the firing circuit.}
{212}{}{Hit boom ball with rock.}
{213}{}{Leave it alone.}
{215}{}{Some instinct older than language stays your hand. The rock, relieved, goes back in the puddle.}

# Behind the plate: dormant, a Mentat taken, rigged, disarmed.
{220}{}{Inside: a nest of cloth-wrapped wire, a row of corroded relays, and one empty socket the size of a fist. Somebody has been in here before you. They were careful. They also left.}
{221}{}{Try to disarm it.}
{222}{}{Fit Burke's pulse charge into the socket.}
{223}{}{Take Mentats first.}
{224}{}{Close the plate and step back.}
{226}{}{The world narrows to a bright and orderly point. The wiring looks no simpler, but you find you resent it less.}
{230}{}{The pulse charge sits in the socket like a tick on a dog, humming. Its clamps have bitten into the old contacts. It will not want to come out.}
{231}{}{Pull the charge out.}
{235}{}{Nothing in here will ever hurt anyone again. It is a very large paperweight now.}

# The firing circuit: first look at a dormant bomb, at a rigged one, a second look, a dead bomb.
{240}{}{You follow the firing circuit relay by relay to the place where the initiator ought to be. It is not there. Without one, this weapon could sit here until the sun burns out. With one, it would need about a second.}
{241}{}{You follow the firing circuit relay by relay to the initiator. It is the pulse charge. Somebody has handed this bomb the one part it was missing.}
{242}{}{You have been over this circuit already. It has not grown an initiator since.}
{243}{}{There is no firing circuit left to trace. Somebody has cut it into short lengths.}

# Outcomes: disarmed, charge pulled, charge fitted, the jolt, close but no, hopeless (disarm, fit, pull).
{250}{}{You isolate the firing relays one at a time and cut the last live lead. The tick stumbles, slows, and stops. In a century and a half nobody has heard this crater so quiet.}
{251}{}{You bleed the capacitor through a wet rag, wait for the hum to die, and lever the clamps apart. The charge drops into your hand, still warm. Then, since you are here, you cut everything else that looks important. The ticking stops.}
{252}{}{The charge seats itself in the socket with a click you feel in your teeth. The clamps close on their own. The tick goes on as before, but now something is listening to it.}
{253}{}{Your hand slips. Something inside the casing bites back: a hard blue snap, a stink of burnt hair, and the access plate slams shut on its spring.}
{254}{}{You get as far as the relays before you have to admit you are guessing. You are close. Not close enough to bet a town on.}
{255}{}{You have no idea where to start. It is a wall of wire. Moira Brown at Craterside Supply is said to have had this plate off and lived. She may have written something down.}
{256}{}{The charge will not seat. There is a trick to the contacts and you do not know it. The woman at Craterside Supply who had this plate off before you might.}
{257}{}{The clamps will not let go, and the hum climbs a note when you lean on them. You stop leaning. Somebody who understands this thing had better explain it to you first.}
# The same three with Moira's notes already read: what happened, and (after it, one of 270 to 275) what would help.
{258}{}{Moira's notes lie open on your knee, and it is still a wall of wire.}
{259}{}{The charge will not seat, notes or no notes.}
{266}{}{The clamps will not let go, and the hum climbs a note when you lean on them. You stop leaning.}
{260}{}{[Step back.]}
{261}{}{Try again.}

# Other skills and items.
{262}{}{It is not broken. That is rather the problem.}
{263}{}{You have no reason to do that, and several good ones not to.}
{265}{}{You look for the firing circuit and find only wire. All of it looks equally important.}

# What would help next time, added to every failure (mgbomb.ssl, hint): 270 no notes, 271 no Mentats to hand,
# 275 Mentats in the pack, 272 no tools, 273 the circuit not traced (Science), 274 nothing left to bring.
{270}{}{Moira Brown at Craterside Supply had this plate off once and wrote down what she saw. Her notes would help.}
{271}{}{A clearer head would help. Doc Church sells Mentats, and Moira Brown has been known to hand them out.}
{272}{}{You are doing this with the point of a knife. Proper tools would help: ask Moira Brown at Craterside Supply.}
{273}{}{You have not traced the firing circuit. Knowing what feeds what would help.}
{274}{}{You have brought everything there is to bring. What is missing is the knack, and that only comes with practice.}
{275}{}{There are Mentats in your pack. This would be the time for them.}
